          "source": "SAToplam 2.0 · Topic 6 · Q7",
          "passageTitle": "Quotation — Q7",
          "passage": "The Monster is an 1898 story by Stephen Crane.\nIn the story, the character of Jim, a young boy,\naccidentally damages a peony (a flower) in the\nyard while his father is tending to the lawn.\nCrane depicts the dedication and care with\nwhich his father typically cares for his lawn,\nwriting ________________.",
          "prompt": "Which quotation from \"The Monster\" most\neffectively illustrates that claim?",
          "options": [
            "After some trouble (Jim's father) found\nthe subject of the incident, the broken\nflower. Turning then, he saw the child\nlurking at the rear and scanning his\ncountenance,",
            "\"(Jim's father) was shaving this lawn as if\nit were a priest's chin. All during the\nseason he had worked at it in the coolness\nand peace of the evenings after supper.\nEven in the shadow of the cherry trees the\ngrass was strong and healthy.\"",
            "(Jim's father) paused, and with the howl\nof the machine no longer occupying the\nsense, one could hear the robins in the\ncherry trees arranging their affairs.",
            "\"(Jim) went on to the lawn, very slowly\nand kicking wretchedly at the furt.\nPresently his father came along with the\nwhirring machine, whale the sweet, new\ngrass blades spun from the knives.\""
          ],
          "answer": 1,
          "explanation": "Correct answer is B (Answer Key: Topic 6, Q7). Verified from SAToplam 2.0 Reading real exam compilation."
        },
